esothelioma</article-title><source>Oncogene</source><year>2004</year><volume>23</volume><fpage>6672</fpage><lpage>6676</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15221014</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><label>21.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Suzuki</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Aberrant methylation profile of human malignant mesotheliomas and its relationship to SV40 infection</article-title><source>Oncogene</source><year>2005</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>1302</fpage><lpage>1308</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15592515</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><label>22.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shivapurkar</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Aberrant methylation of trail decoy receptor genes is frequent in multiple tumor types</article-title><source>Int. J. Cancer</source><year>2004</year><volume>109</volume><fpage>786</fpage><lpage>792</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14999791</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><label>23.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ohta</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Thrombospondin-1 expression and clinical implications in malignant pleural mesothelioma</article-title><source>Cancer</source><year>1999</year><volume>85</volume><fpage>2570</fpage><lpage>2576</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10375104</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><label>24.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tsou</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Distinct DNA methylation profiles in malignant mesothelioma, lung adenocarcinoma, and non-tumor lung</article-title><source>Lung Cancer</source><year>2005</yea